7.4. Sections 7.1 through 7.3 do not remove or reduce the defect-reporting
obligations in Section 8. Those obligations apply to Commercial Use whether or
not Distribution occurs.

8. COMMERCIAL USE AND MANDATORY DEFECT REPORTING

8.1. Condition of Commercial Use.

When You make Commercial Use, compliance with this Section is a condition of
the copyright and patent rights granted for that Commercial Use. A material
violation of this Section terminates only the rights exercised for Commercial
Use, including Distribution undertaken as part of that Commercial Use, and only
as provided in Section 15. It does not by itself terminate rights exercised
solely for noncommercial use.

8.2. Reporting Duty.

After obtaining Actual Knowledge of a Reportable Defect during Commercial Use,
You must submit an initial report through the Reporting Channel:

(a) for a Security Defect, without unreasonable delay and no later than seven
calendar days after obtaining Actual Knowledge; and

(b) for any other Reportable Defect, no later than thirty calendar days after
obtaining Actual Knowledge.

An initial report may state that the issue is suspected, that the root cause is
uncertain, or that investigation is ongoing. The report does not need to prove
that the Software is defective.

If You obtain Actual Knowledge during a Commercial Evaluation and later begin
Commercial Use, that knowledge is treated as obtained on the first day of
Commercial Use when the suspected defect remains applicable to the Software You
use and You still possess the technical basis for the suspicion.

8.3. No Duty to Discover.

This License does not require You to conduct proactive testing, fuzzing,
auditing, penetration testing, reverse engineering, monitoring, benchmarking,
or code review; search for defects; reproduce a defect reported by another
person; determine a definitive root cause; or continue investigating after
providing a reasonable initial report.

The reporting duty arises only from Actual Knowledge obtained during activities
You otherwise choose to perform, subject to the final paragraph of Section 8.2.

8.4. Minimum Report Content.

To the extent reasonably known and available, the initial report must include:

(a) the affected release, tag, commit, or package identifier;

(b) the operating system, architecture, compiler, build mode, and relevant I/O
or runtime backend;

(c) whether the affected Software was modified and a high-level description of
relevant Modifications;

(d) the expected and observed behavior;

(e) reproduction steps, a minimal reproducer, logs, stack traces, crash data,
or other technical evidence when reasonably available;

(f) the known or reasonably suspected impact; and

(g) a contact method for reasonable follow-up.

If You later obtain material additional information without conducting a new
investigation solely for that purpose, You must provide it within thirty
calendar days after obtaining Actual Knowledge of that information, while the
report remains open or is reasonably capable of receiving an update. You are
not required to retain logs or other materials beyond Your ordinary retention
practices solely because of this License.

8.5. Security Reports.

A Security Defect must first be reported through a private Reporting Channel.
You must not publish exploit details, credentials, cryptographic secrets,
personal data, customer data, or regulated data in a public issue merely to
satisfy this License.

If no private Reporting Channel is usable but the public issue tracker is
usable, the deadline is satisfied by submitting a public issue containing only:

(a) the affected release or commit identifier;

(b) a statement that a private security contact is requested; and

(c) a safe contact method.

Technical details must then be supplied within seven calendar days after the
Licensor provides a usable private channel.

8.6. Confidential, Personal, and Legally Restricted Information.

This License does not require You to disclose:

(a) the complete source code of a Separate Work;

(b) trade secrets unrelated to the defect;

(c) credentials, cryptographic secrets, personal data, customer data, or
regulated data;

(d) information whose disclosure is prohibited by law, court order, or a
binding third-party confidentiality obligation; or

(e) information that would create an unreasonable security risk.

You must instead provide a sanitized report or the minimum lawful notice
reasonably sufficient to identify the suspected defect. Additional information
may be provided under a separate written confidentiality agreement.

8.7. Duplicate Reports.

No duplicate report is required when the same defect affecting the same
relevant releases is known to You to be publicly tracked or has been
acknowledged by the Licensor to You through a private report or advisory, and
You possess no material new evidence.

If You possess material new evidence, affected-environment information, or a
materially different impact, You must add that information to the existing
report when reasonably possible or submit it through the appropriate Reporting
Channel.

8.8. Unavailable or Unusable Reporting Channel.

A Reporting Channel is unusable when submission is technically or legally
impossible, including because the Official Repository or channel is unavailable,
submission repeatedly fails, an account cannot lawfully or reasonably be
created or used, or access is blocked by a legal or technical restriction.

If a channel can accept a safe text notice but cannot accept attachments because
of an upload or size limit, You must submit the safe text notice by the
applicable deadline and may provide larger materials through a mutually agreed
method. An attachment limit alone does not pause the deadline when a safe text
notice can be submitted.

If the Official Repository and every applicable Reporting Channel are unusable,
the reporting deadline is paused while no usable channel exists. You must
preserve a reasonable internal record under Your ordinary security and
retention practices and submit the report within seven calendar days after a
usable channel becomes available.

8.9. Good-Faith Reports and Classification.

A report that later proves inaccurate does not violate this License when made
honestly and on a reasonable technical basis.

A good-faith decision that an observed issue is not a Reportable Defect does not
violate this License unless the decision was knowingly false or recklessly
disregarded clear technical evidence.

8.10. Report Materials.

You retain ownership of Your Report Materials. You grant the Licensor a
worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free license to use, reproduce, analyze, test,
and modify Report Materials as reasonably necessary to investigate, reproduce,
mitigate, and fix the reported defect.

A patch or other code intentionally offered for incorporation into the Software,
and not marked "Not a Contribution," is a Contribution under Section 11.

8.11. No Duty to Fix or Support.

A report does not require the Licensor or any Contributor to confirm, fix,
disclose, support, or provide a schedule for the reported issue. The Software
remains subject to the warranty disclaimer and limitation of liability in this
License.

